---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'OEM vs ODM vs Private Label | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'OEM, ODM and private label describe different levels of design control. Compare them and choose the right path for your battery brand.';
const articleTitle = 'OEM vs ODM vs private label';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the fastest way to launch a battery brand?', a: 'Private label — you brand a battery the factory already makes. It is the fastest path, but the least differentiated: every competitor can source the same platform.' },
  { q: 'Which model gives me the most control?', a: 'OEM — you define the specification and own the design. The trade-off is a longer cycle with sampling and approval gates before production.' },
  { q: 'Can I switch from private label to OEM later?', a: 'Yes, and it is a common path: start private label to test the market, then move to OEM once you have volume and a differentiated specification worth protecting.' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Fundamentals" dateModified="2026-09-03" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>OEM vs ODM vs private label</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">All three models let you sell batteries under your own brand. The difference is how much of the specification and design you control.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> OEM means the buyer owns the specification, ODM starts from the manufacturer&rsquo;s existing design, and private label applies the buyer&rsquo;s branding to a supplied product. Choose by how much specification control you already have and how quickly you need to launch.
    </div>

    <h2>Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OEM</strong> — you define the specification and branding; the manufacturer builds to your requirements.</li>
      <li><strong>ODM</strong> — the manufacturer owns a ready design; you select it, adapt branding and optionally request changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Private label</strong> — a supplied product carries your name, label and packaging.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Which one fits your sourcing goal</h2>
    <div class="grid grid--3">
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose OEM</h3><p>When you have a defined technical specification and need exact control.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose ODM</h3><p>When you want a proven platform and faster time to market.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose private label</h3><p>When the priority is brand identity on an existing product.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2>What stays constant</h2>
    <p>Whichever path you choose, verify the standard family, voltage, capacity, CCA, dimensions, terminal layout, certifications and export terms before ordering.</p>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">OEM battery supplier checklist &rarr;</a> · <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/">Build a clean specification &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>Dingwei Battery view</h2>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP} The program can be shaped as OEM, ODM or private label depending on the specification you already control.</p>
    <p><a href="/oem/">OEM battery programs &rarr;</a> · <a href="/private-label/">Private-label programs &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>Which model fits you?</h2>
    <p>Choose by the asset you already have, not by the size of the first order:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You have a specification or design capability</strong> — choose OEM, because you control the product definition.</li>
      <li><strong>You want a proven design and faster launch</strong> — choose ODM.</li>
      <li><strong>You want to test a market under your brand</strong> — choose private label.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If the answer changes by model or market, run each line through the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/">OEM battery specification guide</a> before splitting the program.</p>

    <h2>The cost of getting it wrong</h2>
    <p>Choosing the wrong model costs twice: either you pay development work that should have been ODM, or you sign an ODM agreement expecting to own the design and later find the manufacturer owns it. The commercial fix is to state ownership in writing — not to assume the model name transfers the design.</p>
    <p>Keep that ownership distinction visible when you compare supplier responses with the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">OEM battery supplier checklist</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <ArticleFaq faqs={faqs} />

  <CTASection title="Choose the right battery program" />
</ArticleLayout>
